<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crotone</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Crotone is a city on the Ionian coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the site of the Greek colony of Croton, founded around 710 BCE. Ancient Croton was famed for its athletes, such as the wrestler Milo, for its physicians and as the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras">Pythagoras</a> and his community. A Roman colony was founded there in 194 BCE; it is now a provincial capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crotone.jpg" alt="Crotone" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RennyT · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crotone_vista_dal_volo_Ryanair_FR5116_(BLQ-CRV).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Crotone is a city and comune in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>. The promontory of Kroton was inhabited in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> by indigenous peoples. The Greek foundation was led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myscellus-of-rhypes">Myscellus of Rhypes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, after the Delphic Oracle told him to cross the vast sea and found Kroton next to the river Esaro. The Achaeans were driven by lack of cultivable land and population pressure. Excavation shows that most existing settlements in the area disappeared, and that Greek settlers married indigenous women from the first generation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Croton became one of the most flourishing cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-graecia">Magna Graecia</a>, reaching a population between 50,000 and 80,000 around 500 BCE. From 588 BCE it produced many Olympic winners, the best known being Milo, and its physicians were the foremost among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. Pythagoras founded his school there around 530 BCE. About 510 BCE Croton destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sybaris">Sybaris</a> with an army under Milo. A revolt led by the oligarch Cylon then massacred many Pythagoreans.</p><p>Croton declined, was captured by Dionysius of Syracuse in 379 BCE and by Agathocles in 295 BCE, and submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 277 BCE. Hannibal made it his winter quarters for three years in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. A Roman colony followed in 194 BCE. About 870 Saracens sacked it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> later conquered it. It became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1861.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city was known as Cotrone from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> until 1928, and in 1992 it became the capital of the Province of Crotone. Archaeology has recovered the ancient wall circuit, enclosing at least 617 hectares, and the layout of the Greek town. The Castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, of the 16th century, houses the town museum, and the cathedral holds the icon of the Black Madonna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capo-colonna">Capo Colonna</a>. The national archaeological museum, founded in 1968, holds finds from the Sanctuary of Hera Lacinia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Crotone” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Raids into Southern Italy went on into the 11th century, and in 982 a Christian army under the Emperor Otto II and the brothers Landulf and Pandulf was defeated at Stilo near <b>Crotone</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Most come from Sardinia, but a sizeable minority were found in Etruscan tombs at Vulci, Vetulonia and Populonia, in Campania and at the Greek colony of <b>Crotone</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nuragic-civilisation">Nuragic civilisation · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1844 Attilio and Emilio Bandiera, members of Young Italy, landed near <b>Crotone</b> with about twenty men, were betrayed and, with nine companions, were executed by firing squad.</blockquote><a href="/movement/unification-of-italy">Unification of Italy · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Near <b>Crotone</b> in Calabria, the forces of Emperor Otto II and his Italo-Lombard allies were defeated by those of Abu'l-Qasim, the Kalbid emir of Sicily, who had declared a holy war against the Germans.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-14">14 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A Roman colony was founded there in 194 BCE, and the town survives as <b>Crotone</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/croton">Croton</a></li></ul></section>
